"An infrastructure dependency graph shows the prerequisites that constrain resource creation, updates, or destruction in an infrastructure plan.\n\nUse when: Review provisioning order and dependency cycles. Explain why a resource waits for another infrastructure operation.\n\nAvoid when: The aim is application traffic or physical placement.\n\nVisual grammar: Nodes denote planned resource instances or resource operations. Directed edges encode depends-on semantics under an explicit orientation. Annotations distinguish explicit dependencies from inferred configuration references.\n\nBuild: Choose the tool, workspace, and plan revision. Obtain dependencies from the actual plan or declare a proposed graph. Identify operation-level differences for replacements and destruction. Lay out topological ranks and route exceptional edges through external gutters.\n\nCheck: A dependency does not imply network communication. Creation and destruction ordering follow the tool’s actual semantics. Acyclicity and unresolved references are checked. Resource identifiers and plan annotations remain clear of edges.\n\nPitfalls: Assuming dependency order guarantees service readiness. Ignoring replacement operations that split creation and destruction into separate nodes.\n\nExample: A subnet depends on its network; an instance depends on the subnet and identity resource, while readiness remains separately checked.\n\nAlternatives: Deployment diagram, Orchestration DAG."
